<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="so4" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>SO4: The Laplace Transform (Unit Step and Impulse Functions)</title>
    <objectives>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    I can use the Laplace transform to solve differential equations involving unit step or impulse functions.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </objectives>
    <subsection xml:id="sec-laplace-unit-functions">
        <title>Unit Step/Heaviside Functions</title>
            <definition xml:id="def-unit-step-function">
                <title>Unit Step Function</title>
                <idx><h>unit step function</h></idx>
                <idx><h>Heaviside function</h></idx>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        The <term>unit step function</term>, also called the <term>Heaviside function</term>, is the piecewise function
                        <md>
                            u(t) = 
                            \begin{cases}
                                0 \amp t \lt 0\\
                                1 \amp t \geq 0
                            \end{cases}
                        </md>
                        This can be generalized as
                        <md>
                            u(t-a) = 
                            \begin{cases}
                                0 \amp t \lt a \\
                                1 \amp t \geq a
                            \end{cases}
                        </md>
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        Other notations for <m>u(t-a)</m> are <m>u_a(t)</m> or <m>H(t-a)</m>. 
                    </p>
                    <figure xml:id="so4-heaviside-1">
                        <image width = "60%">
                            <xi:include href="img/so4-heaviside-1.xml"/>
                        </image>
                        <caption>The graph of a general Heaviside function, <m>u(t-a)</m></caption>
                    </figure>
                </statement>
            </definition>
            
            <note>
                <p>
                    The Heaviside function <m>u(t-a)</m> can be thought of as turning an object <q>on</q> at the input <m>a</m>.
                </p>
            </note>

            <example>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        We can write functions in terms of the Heaviside function. Consider the function 
                        <md>
                            f(t) = 
                            \begin{cases}
                                0 \amp t \lt 1 \\
                                t \amp t \geq 1
                            \end{cases}
                        </md>
                        The graph of the function is given below.
                    </p>
                    <figure xml:id="so4-heaviside-2">
                        <image width="60%">
                            <xi:include href="img/so4-heaviside-2.xml"/>
                        </image>
                        <caption>The graph of <m>f(t)</m></caption>
                    </figure>
                    <p>
                        The first part of the function behaves like <m>u(t-1)</m>, but we must contend with the second part of the definition. Since <m>1\cdot t = t</m>, we can write
                        <md>
                            f(t) = tu_1(t)
                        </md>
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </example>
            <example>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Suppose a capacitor takes two seconds to charge, discharges immediately, and does not charge again. Graphically, we have the image below.
                    </p>
                    <figure xml:id="so4-heaviside-3">
                        <image width="60%">
                            <xi:include href="./img/so4-heaviside-3.xml"/>
                        </image>
                        <caption>The graph of a charging and discharging capacitor</caption>
                    </figure>
                    <p>
                        Let <m>C(t)</m> be the function describing the charge of the capacitor at time <m>t</m>. Since we want <m>C(t)</m> to be equal to <m>t</m> on <m>[0,2]</m>, we have
                        <md>
                            C(t) = 
                            \begin{cases}
                                t \amp 0 \geq t \lt 2 \\
                                0 \amp t\geq 2
                            \end{cases}
                        </md>
                        We can start by writing <m>u(2-t)</m>, whose graph is given below: 
                    </p>
                    <figure xml:id="so4-heaviside-4">
                        <image width="60%">
                            <xi:include href="img/so4-heaviside-4.xml"/>
                        </image>
                        <caption>The graph of <m>u(2-t)</m></caption>
                    </figure>
                    <p>
                        Now, we can write <m>C(t)</m> in terms of the Heaviside function: <m>C(t) = tu(2-t)</m>. 
                    </p>
                </statement>
            </example>

            <exercise>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Write the graph of <m>f(t)</m> below in terms of the unit step function.
                    </p>
                    <figure xml:id="so4-heaviside-5">
                        <image width="60%">
                            <xi:include href="img/so4-heaviside-5.xml"/>
                        </image>
                        <caption>The graph of <m>f(t)</m></caption>
                    </figure>
                </statement>
            </exercise>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-laplace-discontinuous-forcing">
        <title>Solving Equations with Discontinuous Forcing</title>
        
        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    One of the advantages of the Laplace transform is that it allows us to more easily solve differential equations whose forcing terms are discontinuous
                </p>
                <p>
                    The Laplace transform of the Heaviside function itself is not particularly difficult; since 
                    <md>
                        u(t-a) = 
                        \begin{cases}
                            0 \amp t \lt a \\
                            1 \amp t \geq a
                        \end{cases}
                    </md>
                    We have
                    <md>
                        <mrow> \mathcal{L}(u(t-a)) \amp = \begin{cases} \mathcal{L}(0) \amp t \lt a \\ \mathcal{L}(1) \amp t \geq a \end{cases}  </mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \begin{cases} \ds \int_0^a 0e^{-st}\, dt \\ \ds \int_a^\infty 1e^{-st}\, dt \end{cases} </mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \begin{cases} 0 \amp t \lt a \\ \ds \lim_{N\to\infty} -\dfrac{1}{s}e^{-st}\bigg\rvert_a^N\amp t \geq a\end{cases}</mrow>
                        <mrow> \amp = \begin{cases} 0 \amp t \lt a \\ \dfrac{1}{s}e^{-as}\end{cases}</mrow>
                    </md>
                    So <m>\mathcal{L}(u(t-a)) = \dfrac{1}{s}e^{-as}</m>. 
                </p>
                <p>
                    A similar computation shows that for a function like <m>f(t-a)u(t-a)</m>, we get 
                    <md>
                        \mathcal{L}(f(t-a)u(t-a)) = e^{-as}F(s)
                    </md>
                    where <m>F(s)</m> is the Laplace transform of <m>f(t)</m>. <alert>Note</alert>: the definition of this transform <em>requires</em> that we use <m>f(t-a)</m> rather than simply <m>f(t)</m>. 
                </p>
            </statement>
        </example>

        <assemblage>
            <title>Laplace Transforms Involving Unit Step Functions</title>
            <p>
                The above example gives rise to two new transform rules:
            </p>
            <p>
                <md>
                    <mrow>\mathcal{L}(u(t-a)) \amp = \dfrac{1}{s}e^{-as} </mrow>
                    <mrow>\mathcal{L}(f(t-a)u(t-a)) \amp = e^{-as}F(s) </mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
        </assemblage>

        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Consider the differential equation
                    <md>
                        y'' + 4y = f(t)
                    </md>
                    where 
                    <md>
                        f(x) = 
                        \begin{cases}
                            0 \amp t \lt 1 \\
                            1 \amp t \geq 1
                        \end{cases}
                    </md>
                    and <m>f(0) = 0</m>, <m>f'(0) = 0</m>. Transforming gives 
                    <md>
                        [s^2Y(s)-s(0) - 0] + 4Y(s) = \dfrac{1}{s}e^{-s}
                    </md>
                    so that 
                    <md>
                        Y(s) = \dfrac{e^{-s}}{s(s^2+4)}
                    </md>
                </p>
                <p>
                    For decomposition, we want to treat the fraction as
                    <md>
                        Y(s) = \dfrac{1}{s(s^2+4)}e^{-s}
                    </md>
                    so that we get 
                    <md>
                        Y(s) = \lrpar{\dfrac{1/4}{s} - \dfrac{1}{4} \dfrac{s}{s^2 + 4}}e^{-s}
                    </md>
                    From the equations above, we get
                    <md>
                        y(x) = \dfrac{1}{4}u(t-1)(1-\cos (2(t-1)))
                    </md>
                    which can be rewritten in piecewise form as
                    <md>
                        y(x) = 
                        \begin{cases}
                            0 \amp t \lt 1 \\
                            \dfrac{1}{4} (1-\cos(2(t-1))) \amp t \geq 1
                        \end{cases}
                    </md>
                </p>
                <p>
                    We can interpret this solution as saying that until <m>t=1</m>, the system is <q>switched off</q>, but at <m>t=1</m>, the system is <q>switched on</q> and begins oscillating. 
                </p>
            </statement>
        </example>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-laplace-impulse-functions">
        <title>Impulse/Delta Functions</title>
        
        <definition xml:id="def-impulse-function">
            <title>Impulse/Delta Functions</title>
            <idx><h>impulse function</h></idx>
            <idx><h>delta function</h></idx>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The <term>impulse function</term>, also known as the <term>Dirac delta</term>, is a <q>function</q> which describes a force applied at a specific moment in time. <m>\delta</m> has two key properties
                    <md>
                        \int_{-\infty}^\infty \delta(t-t_0) = 1
                    </md>
                    and
                    <md>
                        \delta(t-t_0) = 0 \text{ when } t\neq t_0
                    </md>
                    
                    
                </p>
            </statement>
        </definition>

        <remark>
            <p>
                An impulse function isn't really a function at all; technically, it is a <em>generalized function</em>, objects which are far beyond the scope of this course. It suffices to say that <m>\delta</m> is an object which models a momentary force applied to the system modeled by the differential equation.
            </p>
        </remark>

        <assemblage>
            <title>Laplace Transforms Involving Unit Impulse</title>
            <p>
                There are two transforms associated with the Dirac delta:
                <md>
                    <mrow>\mathcal{L}(\delta (t-t_0)) \amp = e^{-st_0}</mrow>
                    <mrow>\mathcal{L}(f(t)\delta(t-t_0)) \amp = f(t_0)e^{-st_0} </mrow>
                </md>
            </p>
        </assemblage>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-laplace-impulse-forcing">
        <title>Solving Equations with Impulse-like Forcing</title>
        
        <example>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Consider the equation
                    <md>
                        y'' + \pi^2 y = \delta (t-1)
                    </md>
                    with <m>y(0) = 1</m> and <m>y'(0) = 0</m>. Transforming the equation gives
                    <md>
                        [s^2Y(s) - s] + \pi^2 Y(s) = e^{-s}
                    </md>
                    which yields 
                    <md>
                        Y(s) = \dfrac{s+e^{-s}}{s^2 + \pi^2}
                    </md>
                </p>
                <p>
                    Rewrite <m>Y(s)</m> as 
                    <md>
                        \dfrac{s}{s^2 + \pi^2} + \dfrac{1}{\pi} \dfrac{\pi}{s^2 + \pi^2}e^{-s}
                    </md>
                    Transforming back to the time domain gives 
                    <md>
                        y(t) = \cos(\pi t) + \dfrac{1}{\pi}\sin(\pi(t-1))u(t-1)
                    </md>
                </p>
                <p>
                    The physical interpretation is that the system behaves like <m>\cos (\pi t)</m> for 1 time unit (say, seconds) until the force is applied. After the force is applied, the system never quite recovers, as it behaves like 
                    <md>
                        \cos(\pi t) + \dfrac{1}{\pi}\sin(\pi(t-1))
                    </md>
                    from that point on.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </example>
    </subsection>
</section>